 per pound before i get  the turkey out you want to get a few  things ready to go make sure you have a  garbage nearby a pair of kitchen shears  a plastic bag and a fresh baking tray  with a rack on it so you want to cut the  plastic off the bird making sure not to  cut the skin or flush of the turkey and  remove the turkey from the whole bag and  then take that baking tray away with the  bag and replace it with the fresh baking  tray with the rack on it so then we're  gonna get our bag ready to go and we're  gonna start cutting off any excess that  we definitely aren't gonna use if  there's extra skin around the neck you  can go ahead and cut that off then turn  your bird around and if the turkey legs  are secured in that plastic holder go  ahead and release them so that you can  easily get into the cavity so then  you're going to reach in and grab any  innards or a bag of giblets and some  people use these for gravy but in this  recipe we're not going to so you can  just discard them and then using a  disposable paper towel dry off your bird  by patting it on all sides and all the  crevices lift the legs left the arms and  make sure the whole bird is dry so that  the salt will stick to the skin so you  can start by just sprinkling the salt  all over the bird and again you're going  to want to get under all the arms and  all the crevices in the cavity on top of  all the skin turn the bird over just  salt the entire thing to make sure that  it's coated entirely in salt and then  you're going to put it in the fridge  uncovered for one day if you're going to  brown any longer than that you're going  to want to cover it with cling film  until a day before and then you can take  off the cling film and let the skin dry  out and this process is going to work as  a flavor enhancer it's also going to  allow the meat to hold on to more  moisture and dry out the skin so it's  nice and crispy you're going to want to  start prepping your turkey for cooking a  few hours before you want to serve it  but again that's going to largely depend  on the weight of your turkey before you  get your turkey out you're going to make  a butter mixture and you can put  whatever you want here you want to start  with some softened butter we chose to go  with fresh sage chopped garlic dried  thyme and dried rosemary we used a  smaller amount of dried thyme and  rosemary because those are quite strong  it's going to be a much more powerful  flavor than fresh herbs then you're  going to mash all that together you can  do this a day in advance all the way up  to just before you prep the turkey and  once that's all ready to go just leave  it off to the side and preparation for  your turkey so to roast your turkey you  can use one of the more traditional  roasting pans or you can use one of the  one time use aluminum trays they're  cheap they're easy it's also great if  you don't have a lot of storage in your  kitchen just put a baking tray  underneath it for support a lot of  roasting pans will come with this rack  but we're actually not going to use it  it doesn't allow for great air  circulation underneath the turkey and  leaves for a soggy bottom as well as a  longer cooking time so first we're going  to cut off the wingtips and you're going  to feel for the joint and then cut right  in the middle of that and then put the  wingtips into the roasting pan to use  for the gravy and next we want to cut  off the wishbone and this is going to  make your life a lot easier when you're  carving later and the easiest way to do  that is to cut off any extra skin so you  can see the wishbone and you'll see kind  of this white outline that looks like a  wishbone and then you're going to get in  with a little paring knife and you want  to cut the flesh on either side of the  bone slowly work your way from the tip  all the way down to the two legs and  then you can go in with your finger and  kind of tug at it and it should come  loose and if it's still pretty stuck to  the bones you can also use kitchen  shears to cut it loose and then you can  kind of get your hand in there and twist  the top off and once you release the  wishbone you can throw that in the  roasting tray as well for gravy and then  you can move the roasting tray aside for  now to make more room and then turn the  birds that the legs are facing towards  you if your turkey came with one of  those plastic holders we want to get rid  of that before we cook it so you can  kind of reach in there and give it a  good tug and it should come out pretty  easily so now you want to start  loosening the skin from the breast to  make room for some butter and you're  going to peel the skin up a little bit  and then use your fingers to release the  skin from the breast and you can also  use a rubber spatula instead of your  hands just be careful that you don't  pierce the skin and putting the butter  under the skin is going to keep the  breast moist as well as keep the skin  crispy and it also means we're not going  to have to baste the bird because we  have the butter already trapped in here  and that will again result in crispier  skin once you're happy you have enough  of the herb butter under the skin you're  going to rub the rest of the butter all  over the remainder of the bird and  similar to the other steps you want to  make sure you're getting in every  crevice lift up the legs turn it over  fully season the bird and the herb  butter if you have any remaining butter  you can rub a little bit of that inside  all around the cavity as well and  instead of stuffing we're going to put  just a few aromatics in but not too many  that is going to block the airflow and  this will give it a little bit of extra  flavor while leaving plenty of room for  hot air to flow through during cooking  and then with the rest of the onion you  can chop that up throw it in the  roasting pan with a chopped up carrot  and a little bit of celery as well for  flavor and then empty a carton of  chicken broth into the roasting pan as  well and then to avoid any mess on the  way to the oven you can place your rack  on top of the roasting tray just to  avoid any dripping in your kitchen and  then any juices that came out of the  turkey when you were buttering it can go  in as well this isn't a super  traditional gravy but it's going to give  you tons of flavor and all the drippings  from the cooking of the turkey will drip  into the roasting pan and flavor the  gravy as well you want to place the  roasting pan on the lowest rack of your  oven and then with the turkey on that  baking rack you're going to put that  directly on the oven rack right above  the roasting tray we baked our turkey at  325 for about two hours but what you're  really looking for is to make sure that  the turkey is reading at 165 degrees  fahrenheit before serving it but take it  out of the oven when it reaches 160  degrees fahrenheit and while it's  resting the temperature will continue to  rise an extra five or so degrees it's a  good idea to start checking it about 90  minutes in the turkey is going to cook a  lot quicker in this method because you  haven't stuffed the cavity and there's  more air flow all around the bird you're  gonna rest the bird for about 20 to 30  minutes before you start carving it and  juices might still flow out of it a  little bit so it's a good idea to put  this on a baking rack just to catch any  of those juices and in the meantime you  can finish off the gravy so then we're  going to take out all the larger pieces  that we can grab with tongs and then  you're going to strain the gravy over a  pot and then at this point you're going  to bring this to a boil and reduce that  and let it thicken this stock is good to  use as is and it will soak right into  the turkey but if you want it a little  bit thicker ladle a few spoonfuls into a  small bowl filled with two tablespoons  of flour and really beat it to make sure  that there's no lumps and then you can  kind of g