ittle tricky as well to release the  wings you're looking to find where the  shoulder meets the breast if you're  having a lot of trouble with it you can  just actually use your hands to release  the joint and then once you kind of pop  it out then you can use your knife to  finish the job so if you had any  remaining breast meat that you weren't  able to get off when you cut the breast  off the first time that'll come off when  you remove the wing don't throw this  away just yet inevitably there's going  to be a little bit of meat left on the  carcass after dinner you can always get  the remaining meat off of there and you  can use that for leftovers later or use  the whole carcass in a soup we'll start  with the breast and what we're going to  do is get it back on your cutting board  what you're doing here is cutting across  the grain and that'll make it more  tender and also it's going to give  everyone a piece of the crispy skin  instead of that one lucky person if you  were to cut slices off the bird and you  can set those aside on your platter and  for the leg we're going to cut the  drumstick off of the thigh what you're  trying to do is cut through the joint  which should happen pretty easily but  it's kind of difficult to find so you  can kind of do an exploratory cut and  get through the flush so you can see  where you're going and you can kind of  wiggle your knife to find it if it's  really difficult you should probably  just pick up your knife and try a  different area or once you've cut most  of the meat you can use your hands to  just kind of pop the joint off now you  can put the drumstick on the platter and  with the thigh we're going to cut the  meat off the bone you can turn the thigh  over and see where the bone is and then  cut on either side and then just cut  that into bite-size pieces and for the  wings you're just going to cut through  this joint so you're just going to cut  off the skin and any meat that's on  there and then you can use your hands to  release the flat from the wing and once  you pop the joint you can use your knife  to cut through any remaining skin or  meat and you can cut off any extra  breast meat that was left on the wing  now that you have all the meat on the  platter you can kind of rearrange it and  make sure that it's nice and presentable  and you can put the nicer parts with a  nice brown crispy skin on top maybe the  kind of more random pieces underneath  that and it's a nice finishing touch you  can add some fresh sage if you have any  leftover for color maybe some lemons  it's gonna be a nice way to cover up any  imperfections but also just add a nice  kind of bright freshness cooking a  turkey can be a daunting task but this  recipe is great because it's so simple  it's low maintenance and it's really  delicious now's the best part you get a  douse it in gravy and everyone can tell  you how delicious your turkey is  you did it it wasn't that hard and you  know it's beautiful and everyone's going  to appreciate your work no matter what  and if they don't make them do the  dishes

